body:
## Summary

The PHP extractor wires up `function_call_expression` and `member_call_expression` into `calls` edges, but it never visits `class_constant_access_expression`. That means references to enums and class constants do not produce any graph edges, so constant-heavy codebases show up in graph analyses as isolated nodes that look like dead code.

## Current behavior

Minimal reproducer:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Support;

class TimeConstant
{
    public const int HOUR_IN_SECONDS = 3600;
}

class CacheService
{
    public function ttl(): int
    {
        return TimeConstant::HOUR_IN_SECONDS;
    }
}
```

Running `extract_php()` on this file returns two classes (`TimeConstant`, `CacheService`), one method (`.ttl()`), the `contains` and `method` edges. No edge connects `CacheService` to `TimeConstant`, even though the method body uses it.

This is reproducible on any project that follows the Laravel convention of extracting magic numbers into enum-like classes, but it also hits any project that uses native `enum Foo { case Bar; }` syntax because enum case access goes through the same grammar node.

## Expected behavior

The method should be linked to the class that holds the constant, with a relation label that makes the intent clear (for example `uses_const`). The existing `calls` relation is reserved for callable invocations, so a new relation label keeps the graph semantically honest.

## Proposed solution

Extend `LanguageConfig` with an optional `static_const_types` frozenset (default empty, so other languages are untouched) and populate it in `_PHP_CONFIG` with `class_constant_access_expression`. In the `walk_calls` dispatcher, handle the new node type by reading its class reference (via the `scope` field with a positional fallback, since tree-sitter-php has exposed the child under different field names across versions) and adding a `uses_const` edge when the enclosing class resolves in `label_to_nid`.

The change stays additive. Languages that do not set `static_const_types` see no behavioral difference.

## Notes

I am happy to open a PR (one is already prepared with tests). I also have follow-up ideas for `scoped_call_expression` and `scoped_property_access_expression` that have the same root cause and can be addressed independently.

## Summary

The PHP extractor wires up `function_call_expression` and `member_call_expression` into `calls` edges, but it never visits `scoped_call_expression`. That means static method calls like `Helper::format()` or `Foo::make()` do not produce any graph edges, so code that relies on helper classes or static factories shows up with weaker coupling than it actually has.

## Current behavior

Minimal reproducer:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Support;

class StringHelper
{
    public static function slugify(string $text): string
    {
        return strtolower($text);
    }
}

class SlugGenerator
{
    public function make(string $title): string
    {
        return StringHelper::slugify($title);
    }
}
```

Running `extract_php()` on this file returns both classes, the static method and the non-static method, and the usual `contains` and `method` edges. No edge connects `SlugGenerator` or its `.make()` method to `StringHelper`, even though the body of `make()` obviously uses it.

## Expected behavior

The calling function or method should be linked to the class that owns the static method, with a relation label that keeps it distinct from instance-level `calls`. For example `uses_static` makes the intent obvious without polluting `calls`, which currently means "invoked a callable through `()` or `->`".

## Proposed solution

Extend `LanguageConfig` with an optional `static_call_types` frozenset and populate it in `_PHP_CONFIG` with `scoped_call_expression`. In the `walk_calls` dispatcher, handle the new node type by reading its class reference (via the `scope` field with a positional name fallback, since tree-sitter-php has exposed the class name under different field names across versions) and adding a `uses_static` edge when the class resolves in `label_to_nid`.

The change is additive. Languages that do not set `static_call_types` see no behavioral difference. I already have a ready patch with tests.

## Notes

This is one of a small family of missing-edge issues in the PHP extractor. A separate issue (and PR) exists for `class_constant_access_expression`, and I am planning one more for `scoped_property_access_expression`. They all share the same shape, so the fixes are self-contained and reviewable in isolation.

## Summary

The PHP extractor wires up `function_call_expression` and `member_call_expression` into `calls` edges, but it never visits `scoped_property_access_expression`. That means references to static properties such as `DefaultPalette::$primary` or `Config::$default` do not produce any graph edges, so classes that expose configuration or theme defaults through public static properties disappear from the graph even when they are read heavily.

## Current behavior

Minimal reproducer:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Theme;

class DefaultPalette
{
    public static string $primary = '#3366ff';
}

class ColorResolver
{
    public function primary(): string
    {
        return DefaultPalette::$primary;
    }
}
```

Running `extract_php()` on this file returns both classes, the method, the static property declaration and the usual `contains`/`method` edges. No edge connects `ColorResolver` to `DefaultPalette`.

## Expected behavior

The reading function or method should be linked to the class that owns the static property, with a relation label that is distinct from `calls`. For example `uses_static_prop` keeps the intent obvious without polluting `calls`, which is reserved for callable invocations.

## Proposed solution

Extend `LanguageConfig` with an optional `static_prop_types` frozenset and populate it in `_PHP_CONFIG` with `scoped_property_access_expression`. In the `walk_calls` dispatcher, handle the new node type by reading its class reference (via the `scope` field with a positional name fallback, since tree-sitter-php has exposed the class name under different field names across versions) and adding a `uses_static_prop` edge when the class resolves in `label_to_nid`.

The change is additive. Languages that do not set `static_prop_types` see no behavioral difference. I already have a patch with tests ready.

## Notes

This is the last of three related issues for the PHP extractor. The other two are filed separately for `class_constant_access_expression` and `scoped_call_expression`. All three share the same shape, so each can be reviewed and merged independently.

## Summary

Projects that resolve runtime configuration through a global helper function, the canonical PHP example is `config('foo.bar')`, lose all the associated coupling when they are graphified. The extractor sees `config(...)` as a plain `function_call_expression`, tries to map the name `config` through `label_to_nid` (which usually has nothing), and drops the edge. No relationship is produced between the caller and the configuration holder that owns the key.

## Current behavior

Minimal reproducer:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Support;

class Throttle
{
    public const int DEFAULT_PER_SECOND = 60;
}

class RateLimiter
{
    public function perSecond(): int
    {
        return (int) config('throttle.api.per_second', 60);
    }
}
```

Running `extract_php()` on this file returns both classes and the method, plus the usual `contains`/`method` edges. No edge connects `RateLimiter` to `Throttle` even though the method clearly reads `throttle.*` configuration.

## Expected behavior

When the extractor sees a helper call whose first argument is a dot-notation string literal, it should use the first segment of that string as the target lookup key. If the key resolves to an existing node (a class named `Throttle`, a file named `throttle.php`, or similar), an edge should be produced with a relation that identifies the helper (for example `uses_config`).

## Proposed solution

Extend `LanguageConfig` with an optional `helper_fn_names` frozenset (default empty). `_PHP_CONFIG` populates it with `config`. Inside the existing `walk_calls` dispatcher, when a `function_call_expression` matches a helper name, the first string literal argument is parsed. Its first dot-separated segment becomes the lookup key, and the extractor tries both the bare name and a `.php` suffix before giving up. When a target is found, a `uses_{helper}` edge is emitted.

The design is intentionally extensible. Adding more helpers later (for example `route()`, `view()`, `env()`, `asset()`) only needs a wider `helper_fn_names` frozenset and possibly helper-specific parsing rules.

The change is additive. Languages that never set `helper_fn_names` see no behavioral difference.

## Notes

This is the next in a small family of PHP extraction gaps I am filing as separate, focused issues. The others cover `class_constant_access_expression`, `scoped_call_expression`, and `scoped_property_access_expression`. I have a PR ready for this one with tests.

## Summary

Dependency injection container calls of the shape `$this->app->bind(FooContract::class, BarImpl::class)` (or `singleton`, `scoped`, `instance`) express the core of a project's runtime wiring, but the extractor does not produce any edge for them. Reviewers cannot see from the graph which implementation a contract resolves to.

## Current behavior

Minimal reproducer:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Providers;

class PaymentGateway {}
class StripeGateway {}

class AppServiceProvider
{
    public function register(): void
    {
        $this->app->bind(PaymentGateway::class, StripeGateway::class);
    }
}
```

Running `extract_php()` on this file returns all three classes, the method and the usual structural edges. No edge connects `PaymentGateway` to `StripeGateway`, even though at runtime the former always resolves to the latter.

## Expected behavior

When the walker sees a `member_call_expression` whose method name is one of `bind`, `singleton`, `scoped`, `instance`, it should look at the first two arguments. If both are `Foo::class` literals, an edge should be produced from the first (contract) to the second (implementation). The relation label should make the runtime meaning clear, for example `bound_to`.

## Proposed solution

Extend `LanguageConfig` with an optional `container_bind_methods` frozenset. `_PHP_CONFIG` populates it with `{bind, singleton, scoped, instance}`. The existing `walk_calls` dispatcher gains a new branch that parses the first two arguments of a matching call, pulls class names out of `class_constant_access_expression` nodes, and emits a `bound_to` edge when both resolve through `label_to_nid`.

The change stays additive. Languages that do not set `container_bind_methods` see no behavioral difference.

## Notes

This is part of a small family of PHP extraction gaps I am filing as separate, focused issues. The others cover static access (const, call, property), the `config()` helper, `$listen` array mapping and `.blade.php` handling. I have a PR ready for this one with tests.

## Summary

Event-driven PHP projects often declare their event to listener mappings in a static class property. The canonical form, seen in Laravel EventServiceProvider classes but broadly applicable, is `protected $listen = [Event::class => [Listener::class, ...]]`. These mappings drive runtime behavior, but the extractor walks past them silently and the relationships never reach the graph.

## Current behavior

Minimal reproducer:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Providers;

class UserRegistered {}
class SendWelcomeEmail {}
class NotifyAdmins {}

class EventServiceProvider
{
    protected $listen = [
        UserRegistered::class => [
            SendWelcomeEmail::class,
            NotifyAdmins::class,
        ],
    ];
}
```

Running `extract_php()` on this file returns all four classes and the property declaration, with the usual structural edges, but there is nothing that connects `UserRegistered` to either listener. The property array literal is completely opaque to the graph.

## Expected behavior

When the walker encounters a class property whose name matches a configured list (for example `listen`, `subscribe`), it should inspect the array literal and emit edges of the form `Event -> Listener` with a clear relation, for example `listened_by`. Every listener in the value array should produce its own edge.

## Proposed solution

Extend `LanguageConfig` with an optional `event_listener_properties` frozenset (default empty). `_PHP_CONFIG` populates it with `{listen, subscribe}`. The existing `walk()` recursion gains a branch that fires when a `property_declaration` sits inside a class and its property name is in the configured set. For each `event_class => [listener_class, ...]` entry, raw class names are recorded along with the source line in a pending list. After `walk_calls` runs (once `label_to_nid` is fully populated), a short pass resolves the pending pairs and emits `listened_by` edges for those that point to known nodes.

The change stays additive. Languages that do not populate `event_listener_properties` see no behavioral difference.

## Notes

This is part of a small family of PHP extraction gaps I am filing as separate, focused issues. The others cover static access (const, call, property), the `config()` helper, service container bindings, and `.blade.php` handling. I have a PR ready for this one with tests.

# Hyperedge polygons render offset to lower-right on HiDPI (Retina) displays

## Summary

In `graphify/export.py`, the `drawHyperedges` script injected into `graph.html` re-applies the network view transform on top of a ctx that is already transformed by vis-network, and uses `canvas.width / 2` as a center anchor. On HiDPI (Retina, `devicePixelRatio = 2`) displays this pushes hyperedge convex-hull polygons and labels into the lower-right area of the canvas, visually detached from the nodes they should surround. Node and edge rendering are unaffected.

## Environment

- graphifyy: 0.4.10 (installed via `pipx`); same buggy code present on `v4` branch HEAD as of 2026-04-14.
- OS: macOS 15 (Darwin 25.2), Retina display, `devicePixelRatio = 2`
- Browser: Chrome + Safari (both reproduce)
- Python: 3.14.3
- Corpus: ~11 markdown docs producing 3 hyperedges spanning 3–5 nodes each.

## Reproduction

1. Run graphify on any corpus that produces at least one hyperedge.
2. Open `graphify-out/graph.html` on a Retina display.
3. Hyperedge polygons and labels render as disconnected shapes in the lower-right area of the viewport. Referenced nodes themselves render correctly in their force-directed cluster.

Screenshot from my reproduction (text labels redacted — I will attach it in a follow-up comment):

- Left/center: force-directed cluster of a markdown corpus. Nodes and pairwise edges render correctly.
- Lower-right, visibly detached: three purple convex-hull shapes (the hyperedges). Each hyperedge references 3–5 nodes that all live in the main cluster, but the hull polygons render at a completely unrelated canvas position.

## Root cause — two bugs stacked

Looking at `_hyperedge_script` in `graphify/export.py`:

```js
function drawHyperedges() {
    const canvas = network.canvas.frame.canvas;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    hyperedges.forEach(h => {
        const positions = h.nodes.map(nid => network.getPositions([nid])[nid]).filter(p => p !== undefined);
        if (positions.length < 2) return;
        // ...
        const scale = network.getScale();
        const offset = network.getViewPosition();
        const toCanvas = (p) => ({
            x: (p.x - offset.x) * scale + canvas.width / 2,
            y: (p.y - offset.y) * scale + canvas.height / 2
        });
        const pts = positions.map(toCanvas);
        // ...draws polygon from pts...
    });
}
network.on('afterDrawing', drawHyperedges);
```

**Bug A — Double-applied view transform (DPR-independent)**

vis-network's `afterDrawing` event fires with the canvas 2D context already transformed to the network coordinate system (pan + zoom + DPR scale). `canvas.getContext('2d')` returns the same context instance that vis-network just finished drawing with — it still carries that transform. Re-applying `(p - offset) * scale` inside `toCanvas` double-applies the network pan/zoom.

`network.getPositions()` returns positions in network coordinates. Drawing those positions *as-is* onto the callback ctx is the same code path vis-network itself uses for nodes and edges.

This bug is DPR-independent, so it's present on non-Retina displays too — but with a smaller visible effect, which may be why it slipped past testing.

**Bug B — HiDPI anchor error (DPR-dependent)**

`canvas.width` holds the **physical pixel buffer** width (`cssWidth × devicePixelRatio`), not the CSS width. The code uses `canvas.width / 2` as a "center anchor," apparently intending the center of the CSS viewport. On DPR = 1 this accidentally matches the CSS center. On DPR = 2 the anchor lands at `cssWidth` — offset by `cssWidth / 2` to the right; the same math shifts it `cssHeight / 2` downward. This matches the direction of the observed visual offset exactly.

So on a Retina screen the combined error is a doubled view transform *plus* a half-viewport shift toward the lower-right anchor. On DPR = 1 only Bug A remains, with a milder visible effect (slight misalignment rather than fully detached).

## Proposed fix

Accept the ctx from the `afterDrawing` callback argument (it's already in network coordinate space) and draw node positions raw. Remove the `toCanvas` helper and its `scale` / `offset` / `canvas` locals. After this change, hyperedge rendering uses the same coordinate space as vis-network's own node/edge rendering, so it is correct on any DPR by construction.

I'll follow up with a minimal-diff PR. Colors, alpha, expansion factor, font size, label position, and the `afterDrawing` hook are all left unchanged.

## Out of scope / follow-up

A separate visual issue is that 3+ node hyperedges can form self-intersecting polygons because `positions` are drawn in extraction order rather than as a convex hull. That's a different concern and not part of this fix.

## Describe the bug

The community panel sidebar previously had **Hide All** and **Show All** buttons above the community list. These no longer appear in the generated `graph.html`.

Individual community toggling (click to hide/show a single community) still works, but there's no way to quickly hide/show all communities at once.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Run `graphify` on any corpus
2. Open `graphify-out/graph.html`
3. Look at the right sidebar → Communities section
4. No bulk hide/show controls are present

## Expected behavior

Buttons (or a toggle) above the community list to hide all or show all communities in one click — useful when the graph has many communities and you want to isolate a specific one by hiding everything first.

## Local patch

I patched `export.py` locally to restore this. The fix is minimal:

**`_html_styles()`** — add styles for `#legend-controls`:
```css
#legend-controls { display: flex; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 8px; }
#legend-controls button { flex: 1; background: #2a2a4e; border: 1px solid #3a3a5e; color: #ccc; font-size: 11px; padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; }
#legend-controls button:hover { background: #3a3a6e; color: #fff; }
```

**HTML template** — add buttons before `<div id="legend">`:
```html
<div id="legend-controls">
  <button onclick="toggleAllCommunities(false)">Show All</button>
  <button onclick="toggleAllCommunities(true)">Hide All</button>
</div>
```

**`_html_script()`** — add function after the `LEGEND.forEach` block:
```js
function toggleAllCommunities(hide) {
  const items = legendEl.querySelectorAll('.legend-item');
  LEGEND.forEach((c, i) => {
    if (hide) { hiddenCommunities.add(c.cid); items[i].classList.add('dimmed'); }
    else { hiddenCommunities.delete(c.cid); items[i].classList.remove('dimmed'); }
  });
  const updates = RAW_NODES.map(n => ({ id: n.id, hidden: hide }));
  nodesDS.update(updates);
}
```

Happy to open a PR with this if that would help.

